.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt FOLD 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm fold
.Nd wrap input lines to a selected width
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl b | Fl c
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl w Ar width
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Copy input to standard output, inserting newlines when the selected
width would be exceeded.
The default width is 80 display columns.
.Fl w Ar width
selects a positive width.
.Fl b
counts bytes instead of display columns.
.Fl c
counts characters instead of display columns.
.Fl s
prefers a break at a blank within the width.
Default column accounting handles tabs and carriage returns; it does not
query a terminal window size.
.Pp
Read standard input with no files or a
.Ql -
operand; otherwise read the named files in order.
Open failures are reported and later files are still attempted.
Input files remain unchanged.
Byte mode can split multibyte text and is useful when byte positions,
rather than display layout, are required.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on successful processing; nonzero for invalid width/options or
input/output failure. Diagnostics use standard error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fold -w 10 /etc/alpha.txt
.Ed
.Sh ABSD PROFILE
This is the unmodified uutils 0.12.0 utility pinned by the platform build.
The reference describes selected source-supported options; use
.Fl Fl help
for the complete upstream interface.
File access uses the process's ABSD filesystem view, output uses its
inherited stream, and the command acquires no additional authority.
There are no native Unix processes, signals or permission bits underneath
this port.
The command matrix exercises the example forms below; other options
listed here are source-checked, not separately guest-qualified.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr head 1 ,
.Xr cat 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
